WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.763
Here is one line of Java.

2
00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:05.423
count++.

3
00:00:05.423 --> 00:00:07.733
It looks like a single step.

4
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:10.867
But the CPU runs it as three.

5
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:13.361
Read the value into a register.

6
00:00:13.361 --> 00:00:14.004
Add one.

7
00:00:14.004 --> 00:00:15.131
Write it back.

8
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:18.625
Between any two of those steps, another thread can run.

9
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:21.568
Two threads share one counter.

10
00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:22.829
It starts at zero.

11
00:00:22.829 --> 00:00:25.140
Each thread has its own register.

12
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:27.507
Thread A reads the counter.

13
00:00:27.507 --> 00:00:28.537
It sees zero.

14
00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:33.412
Before A writes anything, the scheduler switches to thread B.

15
00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:34.635
B also reads zero.

16
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:36.346
A adds one.

17
00:00:36.346 --> 00:00:38.751
Its register now holds one.

18
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:40.350
B adds one too.

19
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.009
It also holds one.

20
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:43.904
A writes one to memory.

21
00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:46.859
Then B writes one, right over it.

22
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:48.976
Two increments ran.

23
00:00:48.976 --> 00:00:50.422
The counter says one.

24
00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:53.660
One update is lost, and nothing threw an error.

25
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:55.438
This is a race condition.

26
00:00:55.438 --> 00:00:59.147
The result depends on how the threads happen to interleave.

27
00:00:59.147 --> 00:01:04.553
Run it a million times on two threads, and the total comes up short almost every time.

28
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:11.041
The fix is to make read, add and write happen as one step that no other thread can split.

29
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:15.475
An AtomicInteger does it with one compare and swap instruction.

30
00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.680
The same class works from Kotlin.

31
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.344
Or hold a lock, so only one thread at a time can run the three steps.

32
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:25.645
Either way, the counter now adds up, every run.
